# Module boundaries

Golden reference: `src/modules/examples/`.

## Purpose

One folder per feature. Consistent names. Strict layer flow. **The most common AI mistake** is mixing HTTP, Prisma, and vendors in the wrong file.

```txt
routes → controller → service → prisma | src/lib/<vendor>
         ↑ HTTP only   ↑ business   ↑ data & integrations
```

## Naming

| Item | Convention | Example |
| ---- | ---------- | ------- |
| Module folder | plural kebab-case | `examples`, `webhook-events` |
| Files | `<feature>.<layer>.ts` | `examples.service.ts`, `examples.mapper.ts` |
| Router mount | plural resource | `apiRouter.use("/examples", ...)` |
| Exports | camelCase | `listExamples`, `createExample` |
| Log events | snake_case | `request_finished`, `queue_add_failed` |
| Prisma generated | `src/generated/prisma` | do not edit |

`pnpm make:module <plural-name>` scaffolds correct file names.

## Module layout

- Flat files per module — no nested `controllers/`, `services/`, or `dto/` folders.
- Cross-module reuse goes through `src/lib/`, not service-to-service imports.

| Layer | May do | Must not do |
| ----- | ------ | ----------- |
| `*.routes.ts` | `validate*` middleware, `asyncHandler`, auth (future) | Prisma, business rules |
| `*.controller.ts` | Read validated `req`, call service, `sendSuccess()` | Prisma, external APIs, `res.status(4xx)` for domain cases |
| `*.service.ts` | Prisma, rules, domain errors, enqueue, call `src/lib/*` | `Request`/`Response`, `res.status()`, `req.user` |
| `*.mapper.ts` | `toXxxDto(row)` — response projection (optional file) | Prisma queries, HTTP |
| `*.queue.ts` | `createOptionalQueue`, enqueue helpers (optional) | BullMQ `Worker`, HTTP |
| `*.job.ts` | `register*Worker()` processor (optional) | Routes, controllers, `req`/`res` |
| `src/lib/<vendor>/` | Vendor SDK/HTTP, timeouts | Feature business rules, Prisma |

## Allowed imports

| From | May import |
| ---- | ---------- |
| `src/modules/<feature>/*` | `src/app/*`, `src/lib/*`, `src/middleware/*`, `src/queues/*`, same module |
| `*.service.ts` | **Not** other modules' `*.service.ts` |
| `src/lib/*` | npm packages, `src/app/env.ts`, other `src/lib/*` |

## Domain errors

Throw from services; `errorHandler` maps to HTTP. Controllers never branch on status for domain failures.

| Error | Typical use |
| ----- | ----------- |
| `NotFoundError` | Missing resource |
| `ConflictError` | Duplicate / state conflict |
| `ForbiddenError` | Permission denied |
| `ValidationError` | Business rule (not Zod parse) |

## Auth & tenancy

Auth, audit, flags: `platform-patterns.mdc`. Tenant isolation: `multi-tenant-safety.mdc`. API responses: `response-contracts.mdc`.

- Services accept explicit `{ userId?, organizationId? }` — never `req`.
- Do not add stub `requireAuth()` until middleware ships.

## Good examples

```ts
// examples.mapper.ts + examples.service.ts — select + DTO mapper
const row = await prisma.example.findUnique({ where: { id }, select: examplePublicSelect });
if (!row) throw new NotFoundError("Example not found");
return toExampleDto(row);

// controller
const example = await getExample(req.params.id);
return sendSuccess(res, example);
```

## Bad examples

```ts
import { getExample } from "../examples/examples.service.js"; // cross-module service import
if (!row) return res.status(404).json({ error: "not found" }); // HTTP in service
const row = await prisma.example.findUnique({ where: { id: req.params.id } }); // Prisma in controller
return prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { id } }); // raw model to client
```

## Explicit prohibitions

- No nested `controllers/` or `dto/` folders inside modules.
- No manual edits under `src/generated/`.
- No `prisma` in controllers or routes.
- No `res.status(4xx|5xx)` in services.
- No Express types in services.
- No external `fetch`/SDK in controllers.
- No importing another module's service.

## AI guidance

New feature: `pnpm make:module <name>` → [add-feature-module](../skills/add-feature-module/SKILL.md). External vendor: [add-external-integration](../skills/add-external-integration/SKILL.md).
